<p>French President Emmanuel Macron has accepted the resignation of Laurence des Cars, the president-director of the Louvre Museum in Paris, after months of pressure following an October break-in at the museum’s Apollo Gallery. Thieves stole French crown/Napoleonic-era jewels valued at about €88 million (about $102 million), according to multiple reports. The Élysée described her departure as “an act of responsibility,” saying it should help the world’s most visited museum move into a calmer period as it pushes ahead with security upgrades and broader modernization plans. Scrutiny has widened beyond the heist itself. Several outlets say the Louvre has also been hit by labor unrest and strikes, day-to-day operational strains, and infrastructure problems — including water leaks and a burst pipe near the Mona Lisa — along with allegations of a ticket fraud scheme. A parliamentary inquiry cited by The Guardian said it found “systemic failures,” calling the museum a “state within a state,” and adding political momentum for reforms meant to protect collections and restore confidence.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/9Dq8MpqKuNQOdh92zxpQ7HqyE7l_O0kOE3M6NKGW-0ticEBrY2dWCB-AD6NwAFloTUnNZvIWqXBJR12dpqm2V_GZrFn5eG_S48hXFOdAVAIOcF542eICap3ruSFObUkSglyjtpHUnIVxRGeajRICY9LMdlGFVCEphCxwyHAGcJJPjjeHh1xhkmzBGbVB72TQBthAIzrt6xN23K7egms-WQ3e5DMYxWq5VNzhKYre6OHtNSgdFwybrlx0cAAuQoDix9oNN8N9f0Mp63pe5w' alt='File photo: Laurence des Cars at a French National Assembly hearing in November 2025, one month after the Louvre’s Apollo Gallery theft.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Inquiry pressure point: A parliamentary inquiry described “systemic failures” at the Louvre and called it a “state within a state,” sharpening political demands for changes to governance and oversight.</li><li>Leadership timeline detail: The Guardian said des Cars offered to step down soon after the burglary and later appeared before lawmakers at a National Assembly hearing in November 2025.</li><li>Operational disruptions cited in coverage: Reports point not only to water incidents, but also to leaks that damaged “priceless books,” along with staff walkouts/strikes and a suspected ticket fraud scheme.</li><li>What the Élysée says comes next: Macron’s office said the leadership change is meant to bring a “period of calm” and a “new strong impetus” for major security projects and other modernization work.</li></ul><blockquote>An act of responsibility - President Emmanuel Macron (via the Élysée Palace)</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Élysée Palace / President Emmanuel Macron: Macron accepted des Cars’ resignation and said it should give the Louvre room to carry out major security and modernization projects. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/france/article/2026/02/24/louvre-museum-president-resigns_6750841_7.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>French parliament inquiry (as reported): A parliamentary inquiry cited in reporting said it uncovered “systemic failures” at the museum, describing the Louvre as a “state within a state.” (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/24/louvre-president-resigns-jewellery-heist-inquiry-reveals-systemic-failures'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>The New York Times framing (as reported): The NYT described the resignation as the endpoint of compounding management and security problems — including a ticket scam, labor unrest, and water leaks — and said security lapses were seen as contributing to the October heist. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/world/europe/louvre-director-resigns-heist.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/france-s-macron-accepts-resignation-of-louvre-director-after-october-jewel-heist/a-76109480'>France&#x27;s Macron accepts resignation of Louvre director after October jewel heist</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/02/24/g-s1-111343/france-paris-louvre-museum-heist-director-resigns'>The Louvre Museum&#x27;s director has resigned in the wake of October&#x27;s brazen jewel heist</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-macron-resignation-paris-louvre-museum-chief-jewel-theft/'>Macron accepts resignation of Paris’s Louvre museum chief after jewel theft, strikes</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/france/article/2026/02/24/louvre-museum-president-resigns_6750841_7.html'>Louvre president hands in resignation to Macron</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/louvre-president-resigns-amid-string-of-scandals-13983380.html'>Louvre president resigns amid string of scandals</a> - firstpost.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/feb/24/louvre-president-resigns-jewellery-heist-inquiry-reveals-systemic-failures'>Louvre president resigns as jewellery heist inquiry reveals ‘systemic failures’</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/europe/article/3344518/louvre-museum-director-resigns-wake-brazen-crown-jewel-heist-paris'>Louvre Museum director resigns following months of turmoil and jewels heist</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/world/europe/louvre-director-resigns-heist.html'>Louvre Director Resigns, Months After Crown Jewels Were Stolen in Heist</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/frances-macron-accepts-resignation-of-louvre-museum-chief-after-jewel-theft'>France&#x27;s Macron accepts resignation of Louvre museum chief after jewel theft</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/louvres-director-resigns-in-the-wake-of-brazen-french-crown-jewels-heist-and-other-troubles'>Louvre&#x27;s director resigns in the wake of brazen French crown jewels heist and other troubles</a> - pbs.org</li></ul>